## corpus/faults/cast_narrow_sign_trap.lu
//! check: run(exit=trap(overflow))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: type.numlit.cast.narrow, arith.checked
//!
//! A change of sign is a narrowing cast at any width
//! (`[type.numlit.cast.narrow]`): `-1` is not a `u64`, so `v as u64`
//! traps. Until kw03 native and release reinterpreted the bits and
//! printed `-1` (a silent wrong answer: the checked machine and lupin
//! trapped). The bit reinterpretation is spelled `v as wrapping[u64]`.
## corpus/faults/cast_narrow_unsigned_to_signed_trap.lu
//! check: run(exit=trap(overflow))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: type.numlit.cast.narrow, arith.checked
//!
//! An unsigned value above the signed target's maximum does not fit it
//! (`[type.numlit.cast.narrow]`): `128 as u8 as i8` traps. Until kw03
//! native and release printed `-128`, the bits read as signed, where
//! the checked machine and lupin trapped. The same rail is D56's
//! `wrapping[u64] as int` (`[type.numlit.cast.wrap]`).
## corpus/faults/cast_narrow_width_trap.lu
//! check: run(exit=trap(overflow))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: type.numlit.cast.narrow, arith.checked
//!
//! A narrowing integer cast keeps an in-range value and TRAPS when the
//! target cannot hold it (`[type.numlit.cast.narrow]`, K12,
//! wolf-lang#533). kw00's `f9_narrow.lu`: `n + 64` fits `u8` and is
//! kept; `n + 299` does not and traps. The value comes from the run
//! (`env_args().len`) so no tier can decide it at compile time. Until
//! kw03 native and release refused the program ("narrowing numeric
//! casts (range-check semantics)"); the checked machine and lupin
//! trapped, as now every machine does.
## corpus/fs/read_at.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="head=01 at5=567 tell=2 next=23\nat_end=eof empty_len=0 negative=invalid\ntell_unmoved=4 closed=io forged=io\ncleaned=true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.fs.read_at, os.fs.tell, err.union, err.propagate
//!
//! s199 (wolf-lang#426, `[os.fs.read_at]`) — a positional read: the
//! bytes at an offset, and the handle's own cursor left exactly where
//! it was (`pread`). The relations this file pins, on every tier and
//! host:
//!
//!   head / at5 / tell / next   two bytes read forward, then three at
//!                              offset 5; the cursor still says 2 and
//!                              the next forward read is bytes 2..4.
//!   at_end                     a positional read at the size is `eof`.
//!   empty_len                  a `max` of zero is the empty list.
//!   negative                   a negative offset is `invalid`.
//!   tell_unmoved               none of the three moved the cursor.
//!   closed / forged            the family's rule: `io`.
## corpus/fs/seek_tell.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="first_handle_above_2=true\ntell_after_reads=7 read=0123456\nend=10 size=10 at_end_is_eof=true\nback=7 last=789\nstart=2 cur=5 next=56\npast_end=14 past_end_is_eof=true\nbad_whence=invalid before_start=invalid closed=io forged=io\ncleaned=true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.fs.seek, os.fs.tell, os.fs.std, err.union, err.propagate
//!
//! s199 (wolf-lang#426, `[os.fs.seek]`, `[os.fs.tell]`) — a handle's
//! offset can be read and moved. Before this file a program could only
//! read forward from byte zero, so `tail -n 10` of a file read the
//! whole file. The relations this file pins, on every tier and host:
//!
//!   first_handle_above_2   the first open answers a number above 2:
//!                          0, 1 and 2 are the standard streams
//!                          (`[os.fs.std]`), never a file's handle.
//!   tell_after_reads       reads of 3 and 4 bytes leave the cursor at 7.
//!   end / size             a seek to the end (whence 2) answers the
//!                          offset from the start, the file's size, and
//!                          the next read is `eof`.
//!   back / last            a seek of -3 from the end, then a read, is
//!                          the last three bytes.
//!   start / cur / next     whence 0 is from the start, whence 1 from
//!                          the cursor.
## corpus/grammar/attr_implemented_set.lu
