## corpus/memory/raw_ptr_mut_param.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="42\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.unsafe.sig, mem.unsafe.raw.1
//! A module-private function takes a raw pointer by `mut`: the caller's
//! pointer VARIABLE is lent (call-by-reference-result), and the mode
//! says nothing about the pointee. `retarget` points `p` at `b`, so the
//! caller reads 41 through it. Before kw02 the checked machine passed a
//! retagged copy, never wrote it back, and printed 2.
## corpus/memory/raw_ptr_private_sig.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="42\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.unsafe.sig
//! K9(b) = B (STATUS #31; wolf-lang#514): a raw pointer may stand in
//! the signature of a module-private function — every caller is in the
//! same module, the audit granule. Passed by value it is a copy of the
//! pointer and says nothing about the pointee (`[mem.unsafe.raw.1]`):
//! `poke` writes through it. Before kw02 this was E1302 on every
//! machine; the checked machine also froze the pointee at the call, so
//! the write was UB row P2 there while the compiled tiers ran it.
## corpus/memory/raw_repr_c_layout.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="1 0 0 0 2 0 0 0 3 0 0 0 4 0 0 0 5 0 0 0 6 0 0 0\n17 3735928559 34\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: abi.layout.c, mem.unsafe.raw.1
//!
//! kw01 (K4, STATUS #31 — wolf-lang#523, a silent wrong answer at the C
//! membrane): a `#[repr(c)]` struct stored or loaded through a raw
//! pointer has the C layout, `{u8, u32, u8}` at offsets 0 4 8, size 12,
//! so `p[1]` starts at byte 12. At trunk 12a56b22 native and release
//! wrote it packed (`1 2 0 0 0 3`, stride 6) and read `b` from offset 1.
//! The second line reads the struct back from bytes laid down at C's
//! offsets, as a C program would leave them. The checked machine and
//! lupin refuse the aggregate store by name (a raw write of a
//! non-scalar); `repr_c_raw_layout.rs` holds a real C compiler to both
//! directions on both compiling tiers.
## corpus/memory/region_str_view_for_inside.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="regions\nhere\n2\n[here] [here] [gions] [here] [11] [7]\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.region.escape, mem.str.view
//!
//! s207 (wolf-lang#540), the legal side: a `for` piece names its
//! receiver's bytes and nothing else. Used inside the region it is
//! free; taken of a parameter, of a literal, of a literal local, or of a
//! `str` built OUTSIDE the region, it may outlive the region, because
//! its bytes do. The `List` a `split`/`words`/`lines` call allocates is
//! not where a piece's bytes live, so it pins nothing. An `int` drawn
//! from a piece, or an element of a `List[int]`, carries no site. If
//! this file ever starts refusing, the rule has become "a for binding
//! is radioactive" rather than "a piece lives where its receiver's
//! bytes live". lupin agrees on every line.
## corpus/rows/first_list_literal_sum.lu
